00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:07.730
to the Peabody museum collections.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.970
We're here surveying the national birds

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:13.960
of Latin American countries.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.053
I am holding here Golden eagle.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.580
This is a specimen from the United States,

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:25.530
but this species also
found in Northern Mexico,

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.810
in Baja, California, and Northern States.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.620
And it's a declining,

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:33.243
therefore it's on the list of
threatened species in Mexico.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.150
You can actually see
that, unlike Bald eagle,

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.010
the Golden eagle and members of this genus

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:45.190
have feathered tarsus,
the lower part of the leg,

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:46.950
just up to the toes.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:51.950
So this feathering is unique
and I have here another raptor,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.680
this one is a national bird for Panama.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.370
So I'm gonna try to gently lift it.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:05.390
This is a Harpy eagle
and it's a juvenile bird,

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.810
adult would have a big
black band on the breast.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:11.520
And if we can maybe focus quickly on the

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.030
talons of this bird, this
is really the largest

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.497
and most powerful eagle in the America

00:01:17.497 --> 00:01:18.600
and the world.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.780
And these talents here,
the claws are the size of

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.403
grizzly bears claws.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.930
This bird is pretty widely
distributed in Panama,

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:31.760
it's mostly found in the Darien
region in the Eastern Panama

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.450
against the border of Colombia,

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:38.160
because it requires large
swaths of a mature forest

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.460
One pair can occupy a territory
of 25 kilometers squared,

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.600
so, this kind of forester are harder

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.620
and harder for these birds to find,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.860
and they have declined
and even disappeared

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.500
from parts of their range.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.743
But in Panama, it's still
a reliable bird to see.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.750
Then I wanted to show you the spectacular

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:03.283
Resplendent quetzal, at
here's a male from Honduras,

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.850
but this bird is a
national bird of Guatemala.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.950
And what you see here as a,

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.560
what looks like a tail is
in fact, upper tail covered.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.783
So these are feathers
that come over the tail

00:02:16.783 --> 00:02:19.700
and the tail is this black and white,

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.850
much shorter set of feathers.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:27.410
So this is very long tail let's call it,

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:29.930
is in the birds from North of the range.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:34.210
But if you go to countries
like Costa Rica or Panama,

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.650
where this species riches with it ranges,

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:40.070
you see that the tails are much
shorter in this population.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:41.640
One other thing I wanted to point out

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.030
that on this male here,

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.510
the red of his belly is rather faded.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.984
And this actually what
happens when the bird

00:02:50.984 --> 00:02:52.170
is on the exhibit for a long time,

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:56.870
this bird is mounted for
display in for galleries

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:59.900
and has glass eyes, and
clearly was exposed to light

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.000
quite a bit.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.100
So this is a challenge we
face when we exhibit specimens

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.800
in the galleries, but here's
a fresh specimen, a female,

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.150
and you can actually see how
much brighter red her belly is,

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.720
but you can also notice that
structural color is those

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.380
that don't depend on pigments don't fade.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.010
And in this case, it's bright green,

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.063
beautiful preserved color.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:28.003
Then, here is a Toucan, this
is actually a sulfuratus,

00:03:29.396 --> 00:03:33.100
the Keel-billed toucan in English

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.160
and in the country of Belize,

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.160
this is a national bird

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.558
and they sometimes called
it just Billed bird.

00:03:39.558 --> 00:03:42.490
And that's obviously because
of this massive bill,

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:46.240
which in life would be brightly,
rainbow colored with green,

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.250
red orange, unfortunately in
specimens in a collection,

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:51.965
these colors are gone.

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:55.280
So we usually, when we collect these

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.410
record colors with photography

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:59.763
or just the description in the field.

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:05.820
And here we have a specimen
of a Turquoise-browed motmot

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.190
and this bird was chosen as a
national bird by two countries

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:17.500
by El Salvador, I believe
where it's called Torogoz

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:23.610
and in Nicaragua where
it's called Guardabarranco

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.311
in both cases, these
names make a lot of sense.

00:04:26.311 --> 00:04:29.460
In one case, Torogoz it's
from (indistinct) name,

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:35.030
and it's resembles the
triple called at Brit

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:38.950
make in some situations like
alarming when they alarm

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:43.950
and the other name,
Guardabarranco simply refers,

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:48.893
it translates into guard of
the Barranca or the Valley.

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:54.970
They nest in cliffs, excavate
burrows in banks of earth,

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:58.260
and sometimes can nest colonially.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.940
And what other interesting
feature here you see

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:05.620
that the tail has the part
of the, of the vein missing.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:07.990
This is actually a natural phenomenon,

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:12.210
the tail ends with these beautiful
iridescent blue spatulas.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:15.030
And these are the only
two central feathers

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.700
that do this trick.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:18.200
These naturally fall off the bird

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:19.780
doesn't have to pluck this,

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:21.760
but feather starts with a complete vein.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:23.940
So that's kind of an
interesting phenomenon

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.330
only found in motmot.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.750
And yeah, this is a bird

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:30.790
that normally it will be
hard to find in the forest.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:33.960
Most of any species of
motmots can be easily heard,

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.790
but not easily seen because
they sit motion less

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:38.880
and they are sit and wait predators

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.760
somewhere in the forest under story.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:44.170
But this particular Toucan, motmot sorry

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.490
is actually quite common

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.150
and can be found in more open habitats.

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:52.660
I saw my first bird in
the Yucatan of Mexico,

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:56.560
and it was sitting on a
wire of the power line.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.403
So, can be found even
in urban suburban areas.

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:05.303
Then we have this spectacular Mccall.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.560
This bird is a national bird of Honduras.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:14.470
Scarlet macaw has a wide range,

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:17.690
it's found not only in central America,

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:18.810
but also in South America,

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:22.780
but has declined and pet
trade and habitat loss

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:24.690
is some of the reasons.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:27.050
They require large cavities for nesting

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:31.860
and what usually you see flying in pairs

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.940
or small family groups,

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.830
as luckily it's still
relatively easy to find

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.890
in most of the countries,

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:42.290
but in Honduras where
it's a national bird,

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:45.683
it's now restricted to Mosquito region.

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:51.310
And finally rather drop bird,

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.660
this is a national bird of Costa Rica,

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:57.533
and it has a rather interesting name

00:06:57.533 --> 00:06:59.240
(speaking in foreign language).

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.690
It's what I call the
nightingale phenomenon,

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:04.720
where a drab looking bird like in Europe,

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.410
the Nightingales cause a spectacular song

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:09.690
and it's really valued for the song,

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.750
but also it often sings at night.

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:14.450
So it's often at the time when people

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:16.520
are on a date and it's kind

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:21.244
of a romantic scene, nice
song, nice conditions.

00:07:21.244 --> 00:07:26.244
And farmers rely on the fact
that the birds start singing

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:29.230
very intensively at the beginning of rains

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:30.350
when rainy season begins.

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.283
So it's a good time to start planting.

00:07:33.184 --> 00:07:35.767
(upbeat music)

